  "text": "To determine the number of values of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive for which the greedy algorithm succeeds, we need to analyze the behavior of the greedy algorithm and compare it to other possible collections of coins.\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Greedy Algorithm\nThe greedy algorithm for finding the minimum number of coins to make up a value \\( N \\) involves:\n1. Starting with the largest coin (25 cents).\n2. Using as many of these coins as possible without exceeding \\( N \\).\n3. Moving to the next largest coin (10 cents) and repeating the process.\n4. Finally, using 1-cent coins if necessary.\n\n### Step 2: Analyzing the Greedy Algorithm\nThe greedy algorithm will succeed if there is no other combination of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins that gives the same total value \\( N \\) using fewer coins. This means that the greedy algorithm will always produce the optimal solution.\n\n### Step 3: Checking for Success\nFor the greedy algorithm to succeed, the combination of coins must be such that no other combination of 1-cent, 10-cent, and 25-cent coins can achieve the same total value with fewer coins. This is true for all values of \\( N \\) because the greedy algorithm is designed to always produce the optimal solution.\n\n### Step 4: Conclusion\nSince the greedy algorithm will always succeed for any value of \\( N \\) between 1 and 1000 inclusive, the number of values of \\( N \\) for which the greedy algorithm succeeds is 1000.\n\n### Final Answer\n\\boxed{1000}",
